<!-- Visual direction: one ring of spoken words, colored by language family, with the chosen word held in the still center. -->
<main class="ww">
  <header class="ww-head">
    <h1 class="ww-mark"><span class="ww-sr">Water</span><svg viewBox="0 0 120 70" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <line x1="60" y1="24" x2="24" y2="52" /><line x1="60" y1="24" x2="96" y2="52" />
      <circle class="o" cx="60" cy="24" r="18" /><circle class="h" cx="24" cy="52" r="11" /><circle class="h" cx="96" cy="52" r="11" />
    </svg></h1>
  </header>

  <div class="ww-stage">
    <figure class="ww-wheel">
      <svg id="wwWheel" viewBox="0 0 1000 1000" role="group" aria-label="A common word for water in each of 45 languages, arranged in a circle by language family"></svg>
    </figure>

    <aside class="ww-side" aria-label="Language families">
      <h2>Families</h2>
      <ol class="ww-families" id="wwFamilies"></ol>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="ww-explain" aria-labelledby="wwExplainTitle">
    <h2 id="wwExplainTitle">Why the words cluster</h2>
    <p>Related languages descend from a common ancestor, and everyday words come along. English <i>water</i>, German <i>Wasser</i>, Russian <i>voda</i> and Lithuanian <i>vanduo</i> all go back to Proto-Indo-European <i>*wódr̥</i>. Latin <i>aqua</i> became <i>eau</i>, <i>agua</i>, <i>acqua</i>, <i>apă</i>.</p>
    <p>Families also swap in new words for old ones. Hindi <i>pānī</i> comes from Sanskrit <i>pānīya</i>, “drinkable.” Tamil <i>taṇṇīr</i> is literally “cold water.” Malagasy <i>rano</i> is kin to Indonesian <i>danau</i>, “lake.”</p>
    <p><i>Nibi</i> is Ojibwe (Anishinaabemowin), a language of the Great Lakes, spoken in the Great Lakes region long before English arrived.</p>
  </section>

  <details class="ww-credits">
    <summary>Recordings and licenses</summary>
    <p>Volunteer speakers on Wikimedia Commons, including the Lingua Libre and Shtooka projects. Ojibwe: Eugene Stillday, © The Ojibwe People’s Dictionary, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0. Audio trimmed and volume-matched; words and etymologies checked against Wiktionary.</p>
    <ol id="wwCredits"></ol>
  </details>

  <noscript><p class="ww-noscript">Turn on JavaScript to hear the words.</p></noscript>
</main>
